WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.569
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:09.509
In this piece of work, you'll see wondrous
little sequences of animations where a virtual

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:15.519
character is asked to write on a whiteboard,
move boxes, and perform different kinds of

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:16.920
sitting behaviors.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.859
The emphasis is on synthesizing believable
footstep patterns for this character.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.220
This sounds a bit mundane, but we'll quickly
realize that the combination and blending

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.070
of different footstep styles is absolutely
essential for a realistic animation of these

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.070
tasks.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:38.560
Beyond simple locomotion (walking if you will),
for instance, sidestepping, using toe and

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.660
heel pivots, or partial turns and steps every
now and then are essential in obtaining a

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.379
proper posture for a number of different tasks.

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:52.629
A rich vocabulary of these movement types
and proper transitions between them lead to

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:56.570
really amazing animation sequences that you
can see in the video.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:01.859
For instance, one of the most heinous examples
of the lack of animating proper locomotion

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:07.870
we can witness in older computer games, and
sometimes even today, is when a character

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:14.330
is writing on a whiteboard, who suddenly runs
out of space, turns away from it, walks a bit,

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.290
turns back towards the whiteboard and continues
writing there.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:24.300
Even if we have impeccable looking photorealistically
rendered characters, such robotic behaviors

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.050
really ruin the immersion.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:31.490
In reality, a simple sidestepping would do
the job, and this is exactly what the algorithm

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.530
tells the character to perform.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.520
Very simple and smooth.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.100
This technique works by decomposing a given
task to several subtasks, like starting to

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.310
sit on a box, or getting up, and choosing
the appropriate footstep types and transitions

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.310
for them.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.090
One can also mark different tasks as being
low or high-effort that are marked with green

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.090
and blue.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.680
A low effort task could mean fixing a minor
error on the whiteboard nearby without moving

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.380
there, and a high effort task that we see
marked with blue would be continuing our writing

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.600
on a different part of the whiteboard.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.510
For these tasks, the footsteps are planned
accordingly.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:12.510
Really cool.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:17.599
This piece of work is a fine example of the
depth and complexity of computer graphics

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:23.700
and animation research, and how even the slightest
failure in capturing fine scale details is

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.689
enough to break the immersion of reality.

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:30.750
It is also really amazing that we have so
many people who are interested in watching

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:36.239
these videos about research, and quite a few
of you decided to also support us on Patreon.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:41.319
I feel really privileged to have such amazing
supporters like you Fellow Scholars.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:46.799
As always, I kindly thank you for this at
the end of these videos, so here goes...

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.579
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

